# LiteFS on Fly.io

Production deploys to **Fly.io with LiteFS** (replicated SQLite). Read replicas serve normal GET traffic; only the **primary** instance may write to the database.

Relevant config: [fly.toml](mdc:fly.toml), [etc/litefs.yml](mdc:etc/litefs.yml), [app/utils/litefs.server.js](mdc:app/utils/litefs.server.js).

## What needs `ensurePrimary`

Call `await ensurePrimary()` **only in route `loader`s that perform database mutations** (create, update, delete, upsert). This is rare — loaders should normally be read-only.

**Call it on the first line of the loader**, before any reads or other work. If a replica handles the request first, any data fetched before `ensurePrimary()` would be fetched again after fly-replay on the primary — wasting resources on both instances.

```js
import { ensurePrimary } from '#/utils/litefs.server';

export async function loader({ request }) {
  await ensurePrimary();

  const session = await prisma.session.findUnique(/* ... */);
  await prisma.session.update(/* ... */);

  return data;
}
```

## What does not need `ensurePrimary`

- **Read-only loaders** — no call needed; replicas can serve reads.
- **`action` handlers** — writes are routed to the primary by the LiteFS proxy configured in [etc/litefs.yml](mdc:etc/litefs.yml). Do not add `ensurePrimary()` to actions.

Prefer moving mutations to `action` handlers so LiteFS infra handles primary routing automatically.

## Do not swallow replay responses

`ensurePrimary()` throws a `Response` with a `fly-replay` header when the current instance is not primary. React Router catches this and replays the request on the primary. **Do not catch or suppress this throw** in route `catch` blocks — re-throw if the caught value is a `Response`.

## Local development

LiteFS is not active locally; `ensurePrimary()` is effectively a no-op in dev. Still add it in mutating loaders so Fly deployments stay correct.
